    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "Strengths of this study include the strong theoretical foundation for investigating disordered eating risk in this population, the extension of a well-established model to include sociocultural pressures relevant to athletes, and a large sample of self-identified athletes, particularly female athletes, participating in a range of sport types and competition levels. Several limitations are worth noting. First, the added sociocultural pressure measures for teammates and coaches have not yet been psychometrically validated. Second, the measure used to assess peer pressure did not explicitly instruct participants to consider peers outside of their teammates, thus there may be some conceptual overlap between the two scales. Third, whether athletes were in or off season was not captured, and this could have impacted the results, particularly if some athletes were relying more on retrospective recall to answer questions about perceptions of pressure. Fourth, the cognitive restraint subscale of the EPSI showed suboptimal internal consistency among men. Fifth, the study was inadequately powered to test moderated indirect effects by gender. Sixth, the study was underpowered to examine the experiences of gender-expansive athletes, including but not limited to transgender athletes. Seventh, despite the absence of identifiable information collected in this online survey, self-report data still introduce potential biases, such as a desire to protect oneself or others within the sports environment."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Future research should extend these findings by exploring contextual and relational factors that may amplify vulnerability to appearance-related pressures, such as team dynamics, shifts in training or competition phases, and exposure to social media, including sport-specific influencers and elite athletes who may promote narrow appearance ideals. Employing advanced methodological approaches, such as longitudinal designs, ecological momentary assessment, and person-centered analyses, will be critical to capturing the complex, dynamic, and individualized pathways through which sociocultural influences, internalization, social comparisons, and disordered eating unfold over time. Ultimately, these insights could inform the development of targeted prevention and intervention strategies tailored to the unique sociocultural experiences of athletes."
